    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i just had to do a rumenotomy on one of my heifers because of a blockage. can u give me a list of what i should be looking for in terms of recovery and signs of infection over the next week? also what should i feed her once she starts acting normal again?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'm glad you're staying on top of her recovery. Within the first week, watch for signs of recovery such as improved appetite, increased saliva production, and normal Bowmans casts. However, monitor for complications like signs of infection, which can manifest as increased rectal temperature (above 101.5°F), decreased blood pressure, lethargy, swelling of the abdomen, or discharge from the surgical site. \n\nAs for post-operative feed, it's essential to transition her back to her normal diet gradually. Offer her dry hay and a small amount of grain for the first 3-4 days, then gradually add silage and concentrate. Around 7-10 days post-op, you can consider regular milk replacer and additional grazing. Consult with a veterinarian for specific guidance on post-operative care and recovery time, as it may vary depending on individual circumstances.",
