    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. she seems okay for now but i'm a bit worried about things we cant see. like if there's something going on deep inside the rumen or at the incision that i might miss before it becomes a real problem. what are the most subtle red flags i should look for?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Even after the initial post-op period, there are still subtle signs to monitor for internal complications. Keep an eye out for changes in rumen motility, such as decreased rumbling sounds or unusual gaseous pressures. Look for any new changes in her stool quality or behavior, like difficulty tolerating food, bovine-kindling (outward strain, or stress), or vocalization. Additionally, the surgical incision site should be clean and dry without signs of discharge or swelling. Any unusual temperature readings or decreased activity should prompt a veterinary visit.\n\nKeep in mind that some signs may take time to develop, so stay vigilant and frequent. Schedule a follow-up check with the vet within 7-10 days after the operation to monitor the overall heifer's condition and assess the healing site.",
